g  efficiencies alone  uh let alone other benefits to the  hospital  so with that like i said i just want to  thank bd for  asking me to speak today and i think we  will  move on to the q a portion of this  presentation  thank you dr maclevania um so  we do have some questions here um and i  just want to remind everyone who's on  the line  if you have a question that you would  like to ask just add it into the q a  the ask a question um chat box um so  the first question that i have here is  from cress  there's four crafts i should say so what  kind of workflow data is available  uh from the keister systems and how do  labs tend to use this in order to  improve  their processes  well first off what uh what keystra  allows us to do  um you know in the first generation with  uh with a daily report  and now with you know the upcoming  generation of of keystream synapses  it really allows us to take a detailed  look at  everything that happens on the actual  system  um with you know taking a look at  at uh in terms of what and you know  what's being processed um  the number of specimens uh  of of each different kind it allows us  to break that sort of thing down  and and really give us a picture of  what's actually coming through the lab  secondly it allows us to take a look at  the uh  you know we can pull information from  the system that allows us to to sort of  give us a life cycle of each and every  specimen to take a look at  when something was was inoculated when  it was incubated um  when was it followed up and what what  that sort of time that that cadence is  how long was something um you know how  long was a specimen  or or a plate ready for reading um and  when was it actually read  right so that's useful in in sort of  helping us compress that that delta t  and and really really you know stay on  top  of uh i guess just the the the mechanics  and the the day-to-day processes  uh that that happen in the lab um  it allows us to take a look at who is  doing what  and how long was was you know how much  effort  we're putting in in different areas of  um  of of the actual uh uh of that that  specimen life cycle  so really what it allows us to do is at  a very level  you know very detailed level it allows  us to analyze the  the you know the basic mechanics the the  operational efficiency of the lab of the  lab staff  and of the work that's going through it  and it allows us  it doesn't tell us how well we're doing  it really just  points us to to ask questions right so  you know  you know looking at one report and one  snapshot of a single day doesn't tell us  anything  if we don't follow and and you know do  that daily management aspect of uh  of of lab management so once we  once we see that something is sort of  you know off  um you know off the charts in terms of  either it's you know  a particular specimen type was was  uh was really either a lot slower than  or a lot faster than usual or  we spent a lot more time working up  specimens it allows us to follow up with  those questions right so again  it's not about the technology and what's  happening it's about how we use the  information that we get from the  technology  in order to make informed decisions  about the lab i hope that answers the  question  yeah i know that was perfect thank you  chris um  question here for dr mcelveen so for  hospitals our labs looking to understand  the financial impact of automation  outside of the lab  what approaches would you recommend that  they take a look at  um so  i guess what it what is the perspective  of  i guess of this question uh is it  someone looking to  bring in automation as you know for  administrators is it a lab director  is it i think it kind of depends but  it is very hard to capture costs outside  of the lab i will say  and so um although we've worked somewhat  um to find find the answer to this uh  you need really strong you need a really  strong data management system like your  electronic medical record to 