 impact we picked  urine because that's what we had the  most of and it's a very  uh it's probably one of our easiest  things to measure but this is affecting  all specimen types that we have  so the the the um thing that i'd add to  that  is is uh you know rachel you mentioned  that  delta t between turn around time uh or  the  between collection and result so that  actually requires us to look outside  outside the walls of the lab what we  found  is that that's sometimes a profound sort  of uh  mindset shift for a lot of labs  uh to really look at you know what  happens outside the lab it does require  us to  to monitor the data but typically what  we find  is that when you start monitoring  something  it does you know it does decrease um  not just magically it's because you know  when we start monitoring it  we actually make an effort to begin to  ask questions that's one of the powerful  things  about automation is the data that it  gives us allows us to  ask questions that will attack those  sorts of things  so turnaround time doesn't magically  decrease because we automate  um but you know there's a there's a  concerted effort  to uh to understand the system and the  process and that's something that  you know as as a healthcare consultant  with bd  we do help labs actually work towards  that  that um you know asking those questions  taking a look at the data and not just  saying well here's your scorecard  is what does that actually mean spending  time in the lab taking a look at what's  actually going on  and what does this metric mean how do we  you know how do we uh  how do we slightly change our behavior  in the lab  and and our workflow in order to maybe  reduce that time right reduce some of  those  some of those delays in between one step  um both  prior to um prior to automation and  afterwards one of the things that we're  looking at is is uh  mapping that out um uh you know from uh  that that's an ongoing initiative to  actually map out that that collection to  result um uh workflow but um but  yeah uh you know really the there is a  reduction there  it's it but again it's not uh it doesn't  just  happen because we uh we flick the switch  on the keystra has to be  managed and and it's important to stay  on top of that  yeah and i would just i i know we're  short on time i would just um  piggyback on chris's answer in that i  completely agree  um you know it allowed us to um  keister allowed us to really look at  what our specimens are coming in  when they're ready to be read and to see  if  we could change our staffing by just a  little bit to  be able to capture a large percentage of  those  organisms um whereas sometimes you know  if if a slight change in staffing  is only built in reading an extra three  percent of cultures that's not worth it  but  maybe an hour or two could get could um  get you  30 of cultures or 20 you know that would  be a staffing change you'd want to make  potentially and so i think it's really  good to be able to track that because  not all  shifts are you know created equally so  completely agree  great thanks so again as you mentioned  we're sort of at the the end of our time  so i just want to thank everybody we do  have more questions coming in um  um what i will do is i will provide the  questions  to the to the speakers and have them  provide a response  so that again don't feel like your  question won't get answered because i  think there's a lot of really critical  questions here obviously this is a  really interesting um topic  so again i wanted to thank our  presenters for joining us today  sharing their experiences and thank you  all for attending  we really appreciate your support  thank you for stopping  great thank you everyone
 

